  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max gets a ride home after a long and exhausting day. He talks about the massive amount of cooking he had to do. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Садись, я тебя подкину до дома.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ну что, как твои дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нормально, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты приготовил новые блюда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, приготовил двенадцать всяких блюд.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Звучит очень вкусно и интересно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я очень старался на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Тебе нравится твоя новая работа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мне всё очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты быстро учишься, молодец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я буду стараться дальше.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Садись, я тебя подкину до дома.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get in, I will give you a lift home.' Vika uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'at home', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, I am very tired today.' Max links 'thank you' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ну что, как твои дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well then, how are your affairs?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'what' straight to 'things'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нормально, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Normally, everything is fine.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'fine' with 'good'. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты приготовил новые блюда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you prepare new dishes?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'of dish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, приготовил двенадцать всяких блюд.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I prepared twelve kinds of dishes.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'dishes'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Звучит очень вкусно и интересно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sounds very tasty and interesting.' Notice how 'sounds' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'interesting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я очень старался на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I tried very hard in the kitchen.' Max combines 'I' with 'kitchen' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Тебе нравится твоя новая работа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you like your new job?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, мне всё очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I like everything very much.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'is pleasing', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты быстро учишься, молодец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You learn quickly, well done.' Vika links 'you' directly with 'quickly' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я буду стараться дальше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, I will try further.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'to try' turns the action back onto the subject. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
